---
const steps = [
 'Start at the orders scan. Its rows are the orders emitted after the date and status tests, not all orders examined.',
 'The outer scan emits 9,176 eligible orders. The inner aggregate therefore runs 9,176 times, once for each order.',
 'Inside each aggregate, the index scan emits about 3 lines per loop. Across 9,176 loops it supplies 27,525 lines; the displayed average is rounded.',
 'The inner aggregate supplies one order total per loop. Grouping adds these by customer, leaving 991 customer totals.',
 'The final sort orders those customer totals. The repeated line lookup, not the small final answer, accounts for most buffer accesses.'
];
---
<figure class="pg-figure pg-walk" data-plan-walk aria-labelledby="pg-walk-title">
 <figcaption id="pg-walk-title"><span>Figure 1</span> A three-row lookup, repeated thousands of times</figcaption>
 <div class="pg-stage">
  <div class="pg-flow-node" data-node="0"><b>Seq Scan · orders</b><span>120,003 examined → 9,176 emitted</span><code>actual rows=9176 loops=1</code></div>
  <div class="pg-arrow">↓ one eligible order at a time</div>
  <div class="pg-repeat" data-node="1"><span class="pg-repeat-label">Repeat for each of 9,176 orders</span>
   <div class="pg-flow-node" data-node="2"><b>Index Scan · order_items</b><span>Find the current order's lines</span><code>actual rows=3 loops=9176</code></div>
   <div class="pg-arrow">↓ lines for this order</div>
   <div class="pg-flow-node"><b>Aggregate · one order total</b><code>actual rows=1 loops=9176</code></div>
  </div>
  <div class="pg-arrow">↓ 9,176 order totals</div>
  <div class="pg-flow-node" data-node="3"><b>Group by customer</b><span>991 customer totals</span></div>
  <div class="pg-arrow">↓</div>
  <div class="pg-flow-node" data-node="4"><b>Sort by value, then customer ID</b><span>991 result rows</span></div>
  <p class="pg-legend"><strong>27,525 lines across 9,176 lookups.</strong> The displayed three rows per loop is rounded; it is not the whole result.</p>
 </div>
 <p class="pg-caption">A row-flow drawing extracted from the measured October per-order report on PostgreSQL 18.6. It turns the native tree upright; arrows carry rows, rather than elapsed time. Costs and intermediate sort details are omitted.</p>
 <details><summary>Figure as text</summary><ol>{steps.map(s=><li>{s}</li>)}</ol></details>
</figure>
